Chapter: ProbeCharLiteral

 A character read out of a text, compared against a character literal. The
 zig plug emits IrCharLit as its raw IR payload -- the CCE code the compiler
 encoded into the literal -- while its char-at answers a Unicode codepoint,
 so the two sides of `char-at s i == 'x'` live in different alphabets and
 the comparison is False for every letter. Bare metal keeps both sides in
 CCE and answers True. The depot's own corpus carries the shape already: the
 vowel-count test is a chain of `ch == 'a'` comparisons.

 The shape is a wrong ANSWER rather than a refusal: both arms compile clean,
 one classifies the character and one never can. Numbers rather than text so
 the answer cannot be confused with a print path. The controls pin the
 failure to the literal: two literals agree on any self-consistent arm, and
 the found-a line must answer 0 on both, so an arm that answers the same
 thing everywhere is visibly not the language.

 Expected on bare metal:

   found-x 1
   found-a 0
   lit-lit 1
   code-of-x <the truth run records it>

 A plug that emits the literal raw prints found-x 0; if its char-code also
 converts, code-of-x moves too.

Section: Classify

  is-x : Text, Integer -> Integer
  is-x (s) (i) = if char-at s i == 'x' then 1 else 0

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("found-x " & show (is-x "axb" 1))
    print-line-uni ("found-a " & show (is-x "axb" 0))
    print-line-uni ("lit-lit " & show (if 'x' == 'x' then 1 else 0))
    print-line-uni ("code-of-x " & show (char-code 'x'))
  end
